{"results":[{"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Demographics_of_Nigeria","final_url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Demographics_of_Nigeria","title":"Demographics of Nigeria - Wikipedia","description":null,"language":"en","text":"From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia\n\n| Demographics of Nigeria | |\n| --- | --- |\n| Population pyramid of Nigeria in 2026 | |\n| Population | 237,527,782 (2025 est.)[1] |\n| Growth rate | +2.1% (2026 est.) |\n| Birth rate | 32.2 births/1,000 population (2025 est.) |\n| Death rate | 11.6 deaths/1,000 population (2025 est.) |\n| Life expectancy | 52.68 years |\n| • male | 52.28 years |\n| • female | 53.07 years |\n| Fertility rate | 4.30 children born/woman (2025 est.) |\n| Infant mortality | 56.68 deaths/1,000 live births |\n| Net migration rate | −0.21 migrant(s)/1,000 population (2022 est.) |\n| Age structure | |\n| 0–14 years | 51.7% |\n| 15–64 years | 45.0% |\n| 65 and over | 3.3% |\n| Sex ratio | |\n| Total | 1.02 male(s)/female (2022 est.) |\n| At birth | 1.06 male(s)/female |\n| Under 15 | 1.04 male(s)/female |\n| 65 and over | 0.77 male(s)/female |\n| Nationality | |\n| Nationality | Nigerian |\n| Language | |\n| Official | English |\n\nHistorical population of Nigeria\n\nNigeria is the most populous country in Africa[2][3][4][5] and the sixth most populous in the world.[6][7] Nigeria is also one of the most densely populated countries in Africa, with approximately 237 million people as of 2025[8] in an area of 923,768 km2 (356,669 sq mi).[9][10]\n\n54.3% of Nigerians are urban dwellers, with the annual rate of urbanisation being estimated at 3.92%.[2][a] Nigeria is home to 250 ethnic groups speaking over 500 languages[2] and the variety of customs and traditions among them gives the country great cultural diversity. The three largest ethnic groups, namely the Hausas, Yorubas, and Igbos constitute more than 60% of the population.[2][11][12][13] The Ijaw, Efik, Ibibio, Annang, Ogoni, Tiv, Urhobo-Isoko, Edo, and Itsekiri are some of the other sizeable ethnic groups.[14] Over 1.2 million people living in Nigeria (0.5% of its total population, or 1 in every 200 people living in Nigeria) are from a continent other than Africa. In particular, 100,000 people are from the United States,[15] 75,000 are from Lebanon,[16] 60,000 are from China,[17] and 16,000 are from the United Kingdom.[18]\n\nNigeria has a young population overall, with 42.54% of inhabitants between the ages of 0–14.[2][19] There is also a very high dependency ratio at 88.2 dependents per 100 non-dependents.[2] The three main religious groups are Muslims (estimated to be 53.5% of the total population), Christians (estimated at 45.9%), and adherents of indigenous religions (estimated at 0.6%).[20] The predominantly Christian Igbo are found in the south-east.[21] Roman Catholicism is the largest Christian denomination in Igboland,[22][23][24] but Anglicanism is also strong, as are Pentecostalism and other Evangelical denominations.\n\nPersons of different ethnic backgrounds most commonly communicate in English, although knowledge of two or more Nigerian languages is widespread. Hausa, Igbo, and Yoruba are the most widely used Nigerian languages. Nigerian Pidgin is used widely as an unofficial medium of communication, especially in the Nigerian cities of Warri, Sapele, Ughelli, Benin, and Port Harcourt.[25]\n\n## Population\n\n[edit]\n\nHistorical population\n\n| Year | Pop. | ±% p.a. |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 1000 | 7,000,000 | — |\n| 1500 | 10,000,000 | +0.07% |\n| 1921 | 18,720,000 | +0.15% |\n| 1952 | 30,403,305 | +1.58% |\n| 1962 | 45,920,000 | +4.21% |\n| 1991 | 88,992,200 | +2.31% |\n| 2006 | 140,431,790 | +3.09% |\n| 2011 | 162,471,000 | +2.96% |\n| 2022 | 227,830,000 | +3.12% |\n\nTotal population of states in Nigeria, according to the 2006 census.\n\nNigeria's population has been increasing rapidly for at least the last 5 decades due to very high birth rates, quadrupling its population during this time. Growth was fastest in the 1980s, after child mortality dropped rapidly. It has slowed slightly since then as both the birth rate and total fertility rate have declined marginally since a 1978 peak. According to the 2017 revision of the World Population Prospects the total population was 191 million in 2016, compared to only 38 million in 1950. Fertility rates turned out to be lower than expected in Nigeria, with double-digit decreases.[26]\n\nThe proportion of children under the age of 15 in 2010 was 44.0%, 53.2% were between 15 and 65 years of age, while 2.7% were 65 years or older. There is a large degree of population momentum, with 3.2 per cent growth leading to the projected population of 546 million by 2100.[27]\n\nThe federal government has not elected to implement the type of controversial family planning programs that have reduced population growth of other developing nations, a result of low political support for these programs and a cultural preference for large families as well as high levels of social instability. Rising educational levels and health care improvements may enable future parents to plan for smaller families.[20]\n\nThe former Nigeria's chairman of National Population Commission, Eze Duruiheoma, delivering Nigeria's statement in New York City on sustainable cities, human mobility and international migration in the 51st session of Commission on Population and Development, said that \"Nigeria remains the most populous in Africa, the seventh globally with an estimated population of over 198 million. The World Population Prospects predicts that by 2050, Nigeria will become the third most populated country in the world. Over the last 50 years, Nigeria's urban population has grown at an average annual growth rate of more than 6.5% without commensurate increases in social amenities and infrastructure.\" He also stated that the population \"grew substantially from 17.3% in 1967 to 49.4% in 2017.\"[28]\n\nPopulation pyramid of Nigeria in 1963 (Hausa)\n\n\n\nView source data.\n\nView source data.\n\nView source data.\n\n### Population by sex and age\n\n[edit]\n\nPopulation by sex and age group (Census 21.III.2006)[29]\n\n| Age | Population | Percent |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Total | 140,431,790 | 100 |\n| 0–4 | 22,594,967 | 16.09 |\n| 5–9 | 20,005,380 | 14.25 |\n| 10–14 | 16,135,950 | 11.49 |\n| 15–19 | 14,899,419 | 10.61 |\n| 20–24 | 13,435,079 | 9.57 |\n| 25–29 | 12,211,426 | 8.70 |\n| 30–34 | 9,467,538 | 6.74 |\n| 35–39 | 7,331,755 | 5.22 |\n| 40–44 | 6,456,470 | 4.60 |\n| 45–49 | 4,591,293 | 3.27 |\n| 50–54 | 4,249,219 | 3.03 |\n| 55–59 | 2,066,247 | 1.47 |\n| 60–64 | 2,450,286 | 1.74 |\n| 65–69 | 1,151,048 | 0.82 |\n| 70–74 | 1,264,937 | 0.95 |\n| 75–79 | 579,838 | 0.41 |\n| 80–84 | 760,053 | 0.54 |\n| 85+ | 715,225 | 0.51 |\n|\n|  |\n\n| Age group | Total | Male | Female | % |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 0–14 | 58,736,297 | 30,462,148 | 28,274,149 | 41.83 |\n| 15–64 | 77,158,732 | 38,348,799 | 38,809,933 | 54.94 |\n| 65+ | 4,536,761 | 2,534,541 | 2,002,220 | 3.23 |\n|\n\nPopulation by age group (estimates 1.VII.2016) (Data are projections based on the 2006 Population Census.)[30]\n\n| Age | Population | Percent |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Total | 193,392,517 | 100 |\n| 0–4 | 31,116,156 | 16.09 |\n| 5–9 | 27,549,964 | 14.25 |\n| 10–14 | 22,221,265 | 11.49 |\n| 15–19 | 20,518,404 | 10.61 |\n| 20–24 | 18,501,820 | 9.57 |\n| 25–29 | 16,816,694 | 8.70 |\n| 30–34 | 13,038,009 | 6.74 |\n| 35–39 | 10,096,763 | 5.22 |\n| 40–44 | 8,891,384 | 4.60 |\n| 45–49 | 6,322,797 | 3.27 |\n| 50–54 | 5,851,717 | 3.03 |\n| 55–59 | 2,845,486 | 1.47 |\n| 60–64 | 3,374,357 | 1.74 |\n| 65–69 | 1,585,140 | 0.82 |\n| 70–74 | 1,832,402 | 0.95 |\n| 75–79 | 798,511 | 0.41 |\n| 80–84 | 1,046,690 | 0.54 |\n| 85+ | 984,956 | 0.51 |\n| 0–14 | 80,887,385 | 41.83 |\n| 15–64 | 107,242,389 | 55.45 |\n| 65+ | 5,262,743 | 2.72 |\n|\n|  |\n\nPopulation by age group (estimates 1.VII.2020) (Source: National Population Commission.)[31]\n\n| Age | Population | Percent |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Total | 206,283,338 | 100 |\n| 0–4 | 32,819,289 | 15.91 |\n| 5–9 | 29,231,173 | 14.17 |\n| 10–14 | 25,970,650 | 12.59 |\n| 15–19 | 20,342,647 | 9.86 |\n| 20–24 | 17,871,826 | 8.66 |\n| 25–29 | 14,992,764 | 7.27 |\n| 30–34 | 13,402,007 | 6.50 |\n| 35–39 | 12,505,764 | 6.06 |\n| 40–44 | 10,427,144 | 5.05 |\n| 45–49 | 7,963,484 | 3.86 |\n| 50–54 | 6,383,640 | 3.09 |\n| 55–59 | 5,002,819 | 2.43 |\n| 60–64 | 3,696,336 | 1.79 |\n| 65–69 | 2,447,988 | 1.19 |\n| 70–74 | 1,591,000 | 0.77 |\n| 75–79 | 915,154 | 0.44 |\n| 80+ | 719,653 | 0.35 |\n| 0–14 | 88,021,112 | 42.67 |\n| 15–64 | 112,588,431 | 54.58 |\n| 65+ | 5,673,795 | 2.75 |\n|\n|  |\n\nHousehold population by age and sex (DHS 2024): [32]\n\n| Age group | Male | Female | Total |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 0–4 | 16.1 | 15.0 | 15.5 |\n| 5–9 | 16.5 | 15.7 | 16.1 |\n| 10–14 | 13.8 | 13.8 | 13.8 |\n| 15–19 | 9.0 | 9.3 | 9.2 |\n| 20–24 | 6.4 | 7.9 | 7.1 |\n| 25–29 | 6.0 | 7.5 | 6.8 |\n| 30–34 | 5.5 | 6.4 | 6.0 |\n| 35–39 | 5.5 | 5.5 | 5.5 |\n| 40–44 | 5.1 | 4.5 | 4.8 |\n| 45–49 | 4.1 | 3.3 | 3.7 |\n| 50–54 | 3.1 | 3.9 | 3.5 |\n| 55–59 | 2.2 | 2.2 | 2.2 |\n| 60–64 | 2.4 | 1.8 | 2.1 |\n| 65-69 | 1.6 | 1.2 | 1.4 |\n| 70-74 | 1.3 | 0.9 | 1.1 |\n| 75-79 | 0.7 | 0.5 | 0.6 |\n| 80+ | 0.8 | 0.7 | 0.7 |\n| Age group | Male | Female | Total |\n| 0–14 | 46.4 | 44.5 | 45.4 |\n| 15–64 | 49.3 | 52.3 | 50.8 |\n| 65+ | 4.3 | 3.2 | 3.8 |\n\n### Population projections\n\n[edit]\n\nThe total population in sub-Saharan Africa is projected to increase to almost one billion people, making it the most populated region outside of South-Central Asia.[33] According to the United Nations, the population of Nigeria will reach 375 million by 2050. Nigeria might then be the 3rd most populous country in the world. In 2100, the population of Nigeria may reach 541 million.[27] While the overall population is expected to increase, the growth rate is estimated to decrease from 1.2 per cent per year in 2010 to 0.4 per cent per year in 2050.[33] The birth rate is also projected to decrease from 20.7 to 13.7, while the death rate is projected to increase from 8.5 in 2010 to 9.8 in 2050.[33] By 2050, 69.6% of the population is estimated to be living in urban areas compared to 50.6% in 2010.[33]\n\n## Vital statistics\n\n[edit]\n\nDemographics development according to the United Nations\n\nRegistration of vital events in Nigeria is not complete. The Population Department of the United Nations prepared the following estimates (*UN World Population Prospects 2022*).[34]\n\n| Year | Population | Live births per year | Deaths per year | Natural change per year | CBR\\* | CDR\\* | NC\\* | TFR\\* | IMR\\* |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 1950 | 36 836 000 | 1,697,000 | 1,010,000 | 687,000 | 45.6 | 27.1 | 18.5 | 6.42 | 184.0 |\n| 1951 | 37 543 000 | 1,731,000 | 1,028,000 | 702,000 | 45.7 | 27.1 | 18.5 | 6.42 | 183.2 |\n| 1952 | 38 263 000 | 1,760,000 | 1,044,000 | 716,000 | 45.6 | 27.0 | 18.5 | 6.39 | 181.6 |\n| 1953 | 38 986 000 | 1,797,000 | 1,056,000 | 741,000 | 45.7 | 26.8 | 18.8 | 6.40 | 180.4 |\n| 1954 | 39 710 000 | 1,831,000 | 1,074,000 | 757 000 | 45.7 | 26.8 | 18.9 | 6.40 | 179.0 |\n| 1955 | 40 455 000 | 1,869,000 | 1,088,000 | 781,000 | 45.7 | 26.6 | 19.1 | 6.40 | 177.9 |\n| 1956 | 41 223 000 | 1,909,000 | 1,109,000 | 800,000 | 45.8 | 26.6 | 19.2 | 6.41 | 176.9 |\n| 1957 | 42 010 000 | 1,948,000 | 1,124,000 | 824,000 | 45.9 | 26.5 | 19.4 | 6.40 | 176.0 |\n| 1958 | 42 817 000 | 1,988,000 | 1,145,000 | 843,000 | 46.0 | 26.5 | 19.5 | 6.39 | 175.1 |\n| 1959 | 43 613 000 | 2,026,000 | 1,159,000 | 866,000 | 46.0 | 26.3 | 19.7 | 6.38 | 173.8 |\n| 1960 | 44 476 000 | 2,072,000 | 1,179,000 | 893,000 | 46.1 | 26.2 | 19.9 | 6.36 | 172.4 |\n| 1961 | 45 381 000 | 2,120,000 | 1,192,000 | 928,000 | 46.2 | 26.0 | 20.2 | 6.35 | 171.0 |\n| 1962 | 46 330 000 | 2,174,000 | 1,214,000 | 960,000 | 46.4 | 25.9 | 20.5 | 6.36 | 169.6 |\n| 1963 | 47 314 000 | 2,220,000 | 1,224,000 | 996,000 | 46.4 | 25.6 | 20.8 | 6.35 | 167.2 |\n| 1964 | 48 332 000 | 2,270,000 | 1,242,000 | 1,028,000 | 46.5 | 25.4 | 21.0 | 6.36 | 165.9 |\n| 1965 | 49 381 000 | 2,320,000 | 1,258,000 | 1,061,000 | 46.5 | 25.2 | 21.3 | 6.37 | 165.0 |\n| 1966 | 50 471 000 | 2,373,000 | 1,281,000 | 1,092,000 | 46.5 | 25.1 | 21.4 | 6.39 | 164.2 |\n| 1967 | 51 570 000 | 2,424,000 | 1,365,000 | 1,059,000 | 46.5 | 26.2 | 20.3 | 6.40 | 165.8 |\n| 1968 | 52 643 000 | 2,478,000 | 1,349,000 | 1,129,000 | 46.6 | 25.4 | 21.2 | 6.42 | 163.6 |\n| 1969 | 53 577 000 | 2,533,000 | 1,367,000 | 1,166,000 | 46.6 | 25.2 | 21.4 | 6.44 | 162.6 |\n| 1970 | 54 945 000 | 2,595,000 | 1,353,000 | 1,241,000 | 46.7 | 24.3 | 22.3 | 6.47 | 160.7 |\n| 1971 | 56 194 000 | 2,671,000 | 1,348,000 | 1,323,000 | 47.0 | 23.7 | 23.3 | 6.53 | 156.9 |\n| 1972 | 57 481 000 | 2,741,000 | 1,343,000 | 1,398,000 | 47.1 | 23.1 | 24.0 | 6.58 | 153.0 |\n| 1973 | 58 866 000 | 2,819,000 | 1,341,000 | 1,479,000 | 47.3 | 22.5 | 24.8 | 6.63 | 149.0 |\n| 1974 | 60 344 000 | 2,922,000 | 1,340,000 | 1,583,000 | 47.8 | 21.9 | 25.9 | 6.70 | 144.9 |\n| 1975 | 61 971 000 | 3,021,000 | 1,336,000 | 1,684,000 | 48.1 | 21.3 | 26.8 | 6.77 | 141.0 |\n| 1976 | 63 731 000 | 3,106,000 | 1,324,000 | 1,781,000 | 48.1 | 20.5 | 27.6 | 6.81 | 137.2 |\n| 1977 | 65 585 000 | 3,203,000 | 1,323,000 | 1,880,000 | 48.1 | 19.9 | 28.2 | 6.86 | 133.8 |\n| 1978 | 67 594 000 | 3,308,000 | 1,340,000 | 1,968,000 | 48.2 | 19.5 | 28.7 | 6.92 | 130.7 |\n| 1979 | 69 673 000 | 3,385,000 | 1,367,000 | 2,017,000 | 47.9 | 19.3 | 28.5 | 6.89 | 128.1 |\n| 1980 | 71 828 000 | 3,460,000 | 1,397,000 | 2,063,000 | 47.5 | 19.2 | 28.3 | 6.85 | 126.1 |\n| 1981 | 74 075 000 | 3,537,000 | 1,426,000 | 2,111,000 | 47.1 | 19.0 | 28.1 | 6.82 | 124.4 |\n| 1982 | 76 276 000 | 3,613,000 | 1,462,000 | 2,151,000 | 46.7 | 18.9 | 27.8 | 6.80 | 123.5 |\n| 1983 | 78 500 000 | 3,704,000 | 1,494,000 | 2,210,000 | 46.5 | 18.8 | 27.8 | 6.78 | 123.1 |\n| 1984 | 80 203 000 | 3,726,000 | 1,524,000 | 2,202,000 | 45.8 | 18.7 | 27.1 | 6.70 | 123.2 |\n| 1985 | 82 472 000 | 3,777,000 | 1,571,000 | 2,206,000 | 45.2 | 18.8 | 26.4 | 6.62 | 123.5 |\n| 1986 | 84 698 000 | 3,824,000 | 1,624,000 | 2,200,000 | 44.6 | 18.9 | 25.6 | 6.56 | 123.9 |\n| 1987 | 86 910 000 | 3,884,000 | 1,656,000 | 2,228,000 | 44.1 | 18.8 | 25.3 | 6.50 | 124.3 |\n| 1988 | 89 178 000 | 3,963,000 | 1,689,000 | 2,274,000 | 43.9 | 18.7 | 25.2 | 6.50 | 124.6 |\n| 1989 | 91 525 000 | 4,080,000 | 1,723,000 | 2,357,000 | 44.0 | 18.6 | 25.4 | 6.49 | 124.6 |\n| 1990 | 93 963 000 | 4,168,000 | 1,773,000 | 2,395,000 | 43.8 | 18.6 | 25.2 | 6.46 | 124.5 |\n| 1991 | 96 465 000 | 4,266,000 | 1,839,000 | 2,428,000 | 43.7 | 18.8 | 24.9 | 6.43 | 124.4 |\n| 1992 | 98 906 000 | 4,373,000 | 1,884,000 | 2,489,000 | 43.7 | 18.8 | 24.9 | 6.39 | 123.9 |\n| 1993 | 101 458 000 | 4,495,000 | 1,922,000 | 2,573,000 | 43.7 | 18.7 | 25.0 | 6.35 | 123.4 |\n| 1994 | 104 092 000 | 4,619,000 | 1,989,000 | 2,629,000 | 43.8 | 18.9 | 24.9 | 6.32 | 122.6 |\n| 1995 | 106 820 000 | 4,732,000 | 2,040,000 | 2,692,000 | 43.7 | 18.9 | 24.9 | 6.27 | 121.6 |\n| 1996 | 109 555 000 | 4,828,000 | 2,081,000 | 2,747,000 | 43.5 | 18.8 | 24.8 | 6.22 | 120.3 |\n| 1997 | 112 357 000 | 4,910,000 | 2,108,000 | 2,801,000 | 43.2 | 18.5 | 24.6 | 6.14 | 118.4 |\n| 1998 | 115 225 000 | 5,002,000 | 2,134,000 | 2,868,000 | 42.9 | 18.3 | 24.6 | 6.07 | 116.2 |\n| 1999 | 118 156 000 | 5,177,000 | 2,138,000 | 3,039,000 | 43.2 | 17.9 | 25.4 | 6.08 | 113.7 |\n| 2000 | 121 235 000 | 5,345,000 | 2,146,000 | 3,199,000 | 43.5 | 17.5 | 26.0 | 6.12 | 110.9 |\n| 2001 | 124 468 000 | 5,496,000 | 2,165,000 | 3,331,000 | 43.6 | 17.2 | 26.4 | 6.14 | 108.1 |\n| 2002 | 127 837 000 | 5,645,000 | 2,192,000 | 3,453,000 | 43.6 | 16.9 | 26.6 | 6.14 | 105.2 |\n| 2003 | 131 329 000 | 5,779,000 | 2,202,000 | 3,577,000 | 43.4 | 16.5 | 26.9 | 6.12 | 102.3 |\n| 2004 | 134 910 000 | 5,902,000 | 2,225,000 | 3,676,000 | 43.2 | 16.3 | 26.9 | 6.09 | 99.5 |\n| 2005 | 138,603,000 | 6,054,000 | 2,232,